# Showcase

These composed interfaces combine public croppers, child stencils, snippets and core algorithms. Open **Show code** for the implementation and helper files.

## Telegram-style editor

Resize the frame and release it: the selection grows back into the available space while the image follows. Try fine rotation with the dial, quarter turns, and screen-relative flips.

<Example source={TelegramShowcaseSource} title="TelegramShowcase" files={{ 'CropperWrapper.svelte': TelegramShowcaseFile0, 'Navigation.svelte': TelegramShowcaseFile1, 'RotationDial.svelte': TelegramShowcaseFile2, 'TelegramCropper.svelte': TelegramShowcaseFile3, 'types.ts': TelegramShowcaseFile4 }}>
	<TelegramShowcase />
</Example>

The toolbar is supplied through a wrapper snippet and the child rectangle owns its stencil options. The demo uses the core's packaged `advanced-cropper/showcase/mobile` algorithms and a scoped corners theme. If you copy the demo into an application, declare `advanced-cropper` as a direct dependency for those imports.

## Twitter-style avatar cropper

Move the photo under the fixed square and use the slider to zoom. `FixedCropper` supplies the frame strategy; an inert child rectangle, wrapper snippet and scoped selectors create the avatar interface.

<Example source={TwitterShowcaseSource} title="TwitterShowcase" files={{ 'Slider.svelte': TwitterShowcaseFile0, 'Navigation.svelte': TwitterShowcaseFile1, 'TwitterCropper.svelte': TwitterShowcaseFile2, 'Wrapper.svelte': TwitterShowcaseFile3 }}>
	<TwitterShowcase />
</Example>

The zoom value comes from the core's absolute-zoom extension. Follow [Absolute zoom](/docs/tutorials/absolute-zoom) to build the slider step by step, or [Styling](/docs/guides/styling) to adapt the visuals.
